    "body": "> Because the meaning of that piece of metadata is exactly that of a poster?\n\nRight, that's the meaning of the metadata. But the meaning of `<video>` with no poster and no video data loaded is a separate question. It seem to me that by omitting the poster attribute, the author has explicitly stated that the element does not represent anything until the video data loads.\n\nFor example, let's say that part of the UI was to display a giant play button when there was no poster. We don't say that the semantics of such a `<video>` is that means \"giant play button.\" We say that it represents nothing, and whatever placeholder UI is chosen is not semantically meaningful, but just a stylistic choice of the browser. They could use a giant play button, some metadata, some ads, a search box asking you to search YouTube for similar videos that might load faster... None of those are part of the meaning of the `<video>` tag as specified by the author of the page.\n\n> By the way, is there another part of the specs or another spec that does specify how a graphical browser is supposed to display the video?\n\nNo. In general, rendering decisions (outside of CSS, which is explicitly designed to control rendering) are left up to browsers. As I noted above, there are a lot of possibilities here, all of which browsers are allowed to implement. This is similar to how there's no specification for how the various form controls are drawn---which has been super important, as it's allowed innovations like special UIs on mobile.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
