    "created_at": "2016-07-22T23:07:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-22T23:07:44Z",
    "body": "I see what you mean, but I disagree with this:\n\n> It seem to me that by omitting the poster attribute, the author has explicitly stated that the element does not represent anything until the video data loads.\n\nIt seems to me, instead, that by omitting the poster attribute, the author hasn't explicitly stated anything. So, if there is metadata that says \"here's a poster image for this video\", for the same reason that a non-playing video with an explicit poster (via the \"poster\" attribute) represents the image in that poster, it seems reasonable to say that a non-playing video with an implicit poster defined in its metadata represents that image.\nThe way an author would _explicitly_ state that the element does not represent anything until the video data loads, would be by specifying an empty poster (i.e. poster=\"\").\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
