    "created_at": "2016-07-25T17:34:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-25T17:34:46Z",
    "body": "The overall question here and in the [Chromium issue](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=629541) is what to do with media resources that have an internal poster image, which is presumably different from the first decoded frame of the video track. Am I correct to assume that no browser handles that?\n\nEven if the \"A video element represents what is given for the first matching condition in the list below\" bit is about semantics, I'd argue it should still change if internal poster images are supported, because that is the only guide to implementors for what to actually render.\n\nMore importantly, these bits would need updating:\n\n> The intrinsic width of a video element's playback area is the intrinsic width of the poster frame, if that is available and the element currently represents its poster frame; otherwise, it is the intrinsic width of the video resource, if that is available; otherwise the intrinsic width is missing.\n> \n> The intrinsic height of a video element's playback area is the intrinsic height of the poster frame, if that is available and the element currently represents its poster frame; otherwise it is the intrinsic height of the video resource, if that is available; otherwise the intrinsic height is missing.\n> \n> The default object size is a width of 300 CSS pixels and a height of 150 CSS pixels.\n\n(Because the dimensions of the internal poster may not match the dimensions of the first video frame, and something needs to define what to do with that mismatch.)\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
